  "summary": "Buddhist mural paintings in the Mogao Caves complex along the Silk Road.",
  "description": "Vibrant, detailed paintings depicting Buddhist deities, stories, and patrons on cave walls, created as devotional art and meditation aids. Developed over centuries showing evolving artistic styles from Central Asian, Chinese, and Tibetan influences.",
  "culturalContext": "The Mogao Caves murals (Dunhuang, 366 CE-1300s, UNESCO) — 45,000m² of wall painting across 492 caves — are the Silk Road's painted encyclopedia: flying apsaras (feitian) trailing ribbons, Sui-Tang paradise sutras, donor portraits of Cao family courts, and Ten Kings' hell courts, mixing Indian, Central Asian and Chinese hands. Cave 220, the Library Cave documents; a 2010s digital-archive boom (Digital Dunhuang) made the murals the most-visited Chinese art online.",
